# PLAN-1079 — 数值：已定宽度、字面量、显式转换（语言共享语料）

The numeric slice of the feature-organized language corpus: the frozen widths
(`int`=i32, `i64`, `u8`, `u32`, `u64`, `float`=f32, `double`=f64), affix-num
literals (`i64`/`u8` suffixes, `0x`/`0b` prefixes, `_` separators), same-type
arithmetic discipline (mixed widths need an explicit `as`/`to`), checked
overflow (never wrapping) and the explicit conversion matrix.

Every case carries ONE Auto source. The `av` check runs the ordinary AV
language chain (`ac-av/language-source-run-v1`, Auto profile); the independent
oracle is the exit value / the frozen trap code, never a value copied out of
the implementation.

## Case: wide-exact
id: "av.parity.1079.wide-exact"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1079", "numbers"]
links: ["PLAN-1079", "PLAN-961"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
fn main() int {
 let big = 9007199254740993i64
 let prev = 9007199254740992i64
 if big - prev != 1i64 { return 1 }
 if 0xFFu8 != 255u8 { return 2 }
 if 0b1010u8 != 10u8 { return 3 }
 if 1_000_000 != 1000000 { return 4 }
 let min = -9223372036854775807i64 - 1i64
 if min + 1i64 != -9223372036854775807i64 { return 5 }
 return 42
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
exit: 42

### Notes

i64 arithmetic is exact well past the f64/double 2^53 integer window, so
`9007199254740993 - 9007199254740992` must be `1i64` (a float-widened or
truncating chain would answer 0 or 2). The literal forms (`0xFFu8`, `0b1010u8`,
`1_000_000`) are the affix-num spellings of the frozen numeric contract. The
last block reaches i64::MIN by checked subtraction and adds back — a wrapping
or clamping implementation answers something else.

## Case: mixed-width-explicit
id: "av.parity.1079.mixed-width-explicit"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1079", "numbers"]
links: ["PLAN-1079", "PLAN-961"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
fn main() int {
 let wide = 40i64 + 2i64
 let narrowed = wide.to(int)
 if narrowed != 42 { return 1 }
 let back = narrowed.as(i64)
 if back != 42i64 { return 2 }
 if (7u8).as(u64) != 7u64 { return 3 }
 var acc = 20
 acc = acc + 22
 if acc != 42 { return 4 }
 if ((0xF0u8 & 0x3Cu8) | 0x01u8) != 0x31u8 { return 5 }
 if (0xF & 0x3) != 0x3 { return 11 }
 if (0b1010 & 0b0110) != 0b0010 { return 12 }
 if 1 + 2 * 3 != 7 { return 6 }
 if (1u32).shl(5u32) != 32u32 { return 7 }
 if (32u32).shr(5u32) != 1u32 { return 8 }
 if 7u32 / 2u32 != 3u32 { return 9 }
 if 7u32 % 2u32 != 1u32 { return 10 }
 return 42
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
exit: 42

### Notes

Mixed widths are a type error in the frozen contract, so the only way to move
between `i64` and `int` is an explicit `as`/`to` (checked narrowing: 42 is
representable). `(7u8).as(u64)` is the zero-extending widening direction.
The accumulator block is the plain-assignment spelling of the same checked
add (`acc = acc + 22`): the ordinary Auto statement surface admits `=`, and
compound-assignment *statements* (`+=`) are a statement-grammar item of the
later pipeline batch (T-01 probe: `acc += 22` is `source.syntax` at the `=`;
recorded as a named boundary in §9, not a relaxation of the arithmetic
contract — the same add is exercised here through `=`).
`((0xF0 & 0x3C) | 0x01)` distinguishes the frozen `&`/`|`/`^` shared tier
(same precedence, left associative) from a C-like tighter `&`: the frozen
answer is `0x31`, the tighter-`&` reading would answer `0x30`. The `0xF & 0x3`
and `0b1010 & 0b0110` rows pin the radix spellings whose bodies are letters or
binary digits only (a decimal-digit-only literal decoder answers
"carries no digits" for `0xF`). Shifts and integer division/modulo keep their
own frozen rules (arithmetic/logical direction, truncating division, remainder
sign).

## Case: overflow-is-not-wrap
id: "av.parity.1079.overflow-is-not-wrap"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1079", "numbers"]
links: ["PLAN-1079", "PLAN-961"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
fn main() int {
 var n = 2147483647
 return n + 1
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
stage: "run"
code: "av.trap.int-overflow"

### Notes

The frozen numeric contract is checked arithmetic: i32 `MAX + 1` traps
`av.trap.int-overflow` at run time instead of wrapping to `-2147483648` (the
legacy wrapping behaviour is a named difference, not the oracle). `var n`
keeps the add out of any constant fold so the trap phase is the runtime phase
the contract names. This is a language trap, not a diagnosed admission error,
so the expected completion is `stage: run` + a trap-code substring.
